<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rabi'a</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rabi'a ibn Nizar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rabi'a was a large confederation of Arab tribes, traditionally descended from Rabi'a ibn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizari-ismailism">Nizar</a>, which included the Bakr ibn Wa'il, Taghlib and Abd al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qays">Qays</a>. Before and after the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> its tribes lived in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, giving their name to the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyar-rabia">Diyar Rabi'a</a>. In the 9th century groups of Rabi'a settled around Aswan and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-desert">Eastern Desert</a> gold mines, where they formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-kanz">Banu Kanz</a>.</p></div>
